<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ralpachen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">reigned c. 815–838</span> · Also known as Tritsuk Detsen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ralpachen, also called Tritsuk Detsen, was emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> from about 815 to 838. A lavish patron of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, he promoted the translation of scriptures and the standardisation of terms, and concluded the peace treaty of 821–822 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a>, recorded on a bilingual pillar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a>. He was murdered in 838 and succeeded by his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langdarma">Langdarma</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetans">Tibetans</a> remember him as the third of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">Dharma</a> kings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ralpachen.jpg" alt="Ralpachen" width="320" height="489" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ralpachen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
